82
00:04:52,720 --> 00:05:00,043
We, we also, I don't know if it's too premature to say, but we also received the funding for next year's round.

83
00:05:00,143 --> 00:05:02,544
So this is a multi year project.

84
00:05:04,604 --> 00:05:07,986
I'll just go ahead and start my presentation to the next slide.

85
00:05:10,607 --> 00:05:14,228
Because I'm having some technical issues

86
00:05:15,141 --> 00:05:26,366
Okay, so this is a multi-year project that we originally had envisioned to essentially, the ultimate goal is to create a culturally competent awareness campaign.

87
00:05:27,166 --> 00:05:35,150
But we knew from the very beginning that there were a lot of gaps that we needed to understand before we got ahead and started that campaign.

88
00:05:35,970 --> 00:05:39,172
And so we essentially started by

89
00:05:39,694 --> 00:05:59,368
trying to build trusting relationships with community, diverse cultural linguistic communities, to really develop a community understanding of behavioral health issues in those communities and how those communities perceive and their attitudes towards behavioral health care.

90
00:06:00,169 --> 00:06:08,715
But with the ultimate goal to minimize stigma of behavioral health and that of course is substance use disorder.

91
00:06:09,447 --> 00:06:13,489
and to normalize and encourage connections to care.

92
00:06:14,970 --> 00:06:34,521
So what we did is we collaborated with diverse cultural linguistic communities in these last listening sessions to really discuss in somewhat of an informal way and an open-ended way the ways in which different communities discuss and perceive behavioral health issues.

93
00:06:35,861 --> 00:06:38,563
But again, we're hoping to re-engage those

94
00:06:38,922 --> 00:06:48,627
community members as well as additional community members to continue this work in developing a culturally competent awareness campaign.

95
00:06:48,947 --> 00:06:59,312
So really thinking through with all the considerations and the things that we've learned from the listening sessions and really integrating that and implementing it for the awareness campaign.

96
00:07:01,133 --> 00:07:07,896
So what we did is we had an implementation team out of the Office of Prevention and Outreach.

97
00:07:08,521 --> 00:07:23,094
who reached out to different communities through community partners and faith-based organizations to really reach our priority populations of five different languages.

98
00:07:23,634 --> 00:07:26,197
So here it's listed.

99
00:07:26,357 --> 00:07:35,645
We spoke with folks from Arabic-speaking community in Medford, Haitian Creole-speaking community in Medford, Spanish-speaking community in Medford, Portuguese-speaking

100
00:07:36,165 --> 00:07:41,950
community in Medford, and specifically Brazilian Portuguese, and as well as English BIPOC.

101
00:07:43,151 --> 00:08:04,349
Additionally, I think it's important to note that we hired session staff to be facilitators who were native speakers in those languages, as well as an in-session note taker to really jot down all the important mentions, and as well as what we call the post-session reviewer, which is really just to make sure that

102
00:08:05,191 --> 00:08:11,034
whatever was captured in the notes was reflected and in case anyone forgot anything live.

103
00:08:13,715 --> 00:08:15,536
So what did we hear?

104
00:08:15,616 --> 00:08:20,479
And I think this is probably the most, what you're all here to hear.

105
00:08:20,499 --> 00:08:21,639
So what did we hear?

106
00:08:22,720 --> 00:08:23,660
The major barriers.

107
00:08:24,180 --> 00:08:29,703
Of course, I think one thing that we have often heard time and time again is stigma.

108
00:08:30,183 --> 00:08:33,465
But it was very interesting to hear how it was broken down into

109
00:08:34,296 --> 00:08:48,218
Three different types of fears, the first being the fear of ostracization from within the family and from within the community, fear of discrimination from providers, and fear of healthcare services broadly and generally.

110
00:08:50,839 --> 00:09:03,521
Another added barrier was limited accessibility, so that is the availability of care, mobility access and affordability access to care, and limited language support and language care.

111
00:09:04,832 --> 00:09:20,895
So this also like sort of ties into unclear communications and supports, that is the limited language, because though some providers did offer language services, it wasn't clear to community members that that was available.

112
00:09:21,055 --> 00:09:25,596
So that's why it's sort of, they're related, but I did put them in different boxes.

113
00:09:26,637 --> 00:09:30,717
But they're also unaware of the health issues that are worth seeking care for.

114
00:09:30,817 --> 00:09:33,098
So I think there is clearly a cultural

115
00:09:33,511 --> 00:09:38,853
connotation of what is a severe enough problem for me to receive help or what is not.

116
00:09:40,213 --> 00:09:47,955
And then the limited understanding of what nearby services are available, uncertain of the medical processes for care.

117
00:09:47,995 --> 00:09:50,396
What does that mean if I go seek help?

118
00:09:51,677 --> 00:09:52,797
What is going to happen to me?

119
00:09:53,817 --> 00:09:55,858
Will I automatically be admitted?

120
00:09:56,358 --> 00:09:56,898
Things like that.

121
00:09:57,498 --> 00:10:02,380
And again, the limited client support to navigate these processes.

122
00:10:04,544 --> 00:10:08,607
Then the benefits that we heard from these communities was this idea of unburdening.

123
00:10:08,867 --> 00:10:33,983
And I put it in quotes because it was actually shared in Spanish as desjugarse, so the unburdening of oneself and of your family, so that you can share your personal aspirations and challenges in a confidential space without the judgment or the ear of someone else that might want to have a say, but also not worrying your family or burdening your family with worry.

124
00:10:34,980 --> 00:10:46,747
and the loved ones that you are seeking help, that for you, it's important for you to take care of yourself, but in a way, it's taking care of your family and your community by not adding to their worry.

125
00:10:48,188 --> 00:11:01,495
Another piece that came up was around personal growth, and I put here self-care in quotes because, and this will come up in a later slide, but self-care was very much referred to as an English term, but that

126
00:11:02,171 --> 00:11:05,053
it sort of resonates in terms of how we understand self-care.

127
00:11:05,093 --> 00:11:21,204
So that they saw that, you know, to go receive behavioral health care, you could really ground one yourself and better understand yourself, that it's a safe space for taboo issues that you might not be able to speak to about with your family or your community.

128
00:11:22,665 --> 00:11:30,390
But all across the board, they all really spoke to how behavioral health care has a lot of benefits

129
00:11:30,903 --> 00:11:46,611
and value for mental health care, and that we should be very explicit when we advertise the service of how it can bring value to someone's life and someone's personal growth, and not just the illness per se or a disease.

130
00:11:47,871 --> 00:11:55,735
And then finally, that the support services, so those folks who were aware that there were language supports, definitely appreciated that.

131
00:11:56,670 --> 00:12:08,860
However, they did add the importance of having a provider from the same cultural background, not just the same language, due to like understanding context, cultural context that they have to share.

132
00:12:08,880 --> 00:12:20,769
And then also that those who had access to MassHealth also had a huge, were very cognizant of that benefit to access behavioral health care.

133
00:12:23,131 --> 00:12:25,973
And then, how many, sorry, why?

134
00:12:26,736 --> 00:12:29,918
That is really, okay, here we go.

135
00:12:29,998 --> 00:12:36,101
So this was the campaign that was presented to the listening session groups.

136
00:12:36,941 --> 00:12:44,225
The one on the left, or your right, actually, I'm not sure how you see this, but there's obviously one in English, and there's one in Spanish.

137
00:12:45,946 --> 00:12:54,811
The one in English describes healthy habits, and the one on the right describes the five signs that's mentioned in the English one as well.

138
00:12:55,828 --> 00:12:57,209
delineates the five signs.

139
00:12:59,109 --> 00:13:04,912
For example, the health habits of take care, check in, engage, relax, and no five signs.

140
00:13:07,293 --> 00:13:11,454
Those were the campaigns that we asked participants to comment on.

141
00:13:13,195 --> 00:13:15,616
And so they shared their input for improvement.

142
00:13:15,636 --> 00:13:20,818
So what I thought was interesting, at least from my perspective of translating materials,

143
00:13:21,586 --> 00:13:38,330
It can be helpful just to have bilingual text that you don't necessarily have to completely translate an entire document without any English or whatever, but that you could have English and then the other language or other target languages that it can sometimes actually help with learning English as well.

144
00:13:39,271 --> 00:13:42,932
They mentioned that the colors that were chosen were very dull and uninviting.

145
00:13:43,772 --> 00:13:48,453
Unfavorable was another term, which they felt sort of added to the stigma even.

146
00:13:49,587 --> 00:13:54,908
And that the smelly face cartoons did not really resonate at all with how they were feeling.

147
00:13:54,928 --> 00:14:01,529
And it would not call to them if they were having an emotional issue.

148
00:14:02,450 --> 00:14:11,912
They also mentioned to clarify the message and the call to action that there was not clear sense, especially in the English one, what the five signs were.

149
00:14:13,712 --> 00:14:18,393
And within that particular promotion material,

150
00:14:19,842 --> 00:14:31,246
each slide should essentially be a standalone slide, even if there was one another promotional material that explained it in more detail that it should really stand alone.

151
00:14:31,266 --> 00:14:36,328
And they were unsure like the purpose of these materials.

152
00:14:37,149 --> 00:14:41,330
Was it to raise awareness or was it to encourage access to care?

153
00:14:41,390 --> 00:14:42,551
It wasn't really clear.

154
00:14:42,611 --> 00:14:45,972
So they did suggest to add a little bit more direction of what to do.

155
00:14:47,318 --> 00:14:51,440
And then the last component here is beware of interpretation.

156
00:14:51,520 --> 00:15:10,909
So we had English speakers in our Spanish speaking group, and they commented that the English material was much more positive and motivating, really would get her to seek care if she needed it, but the Spanish one seemed very negative and very,

157
00:15:13,326 --> 00:15:20,487
sort of like all the negative sides to emotional care and did not really see that inviting.

158
00:15:21,547 --> 00:15:25,268
And again, the term of self-care was not really culturally resonant.

159
00:15:25,308 --> 00:15:38,370
So instead of saying self-care as like a placeholder for many different activities, to just be explicit about what kind of self-care we might be suggesting or offering or giving clear examples really.

160
00:15:40,471 --> 00:15:42,091
And so then what do we recommend based on

161
00:15:42,775 --> 00:15:49,498
on these, what we heard, and these are also directly from the listening sessions that they recommended.

162
00:15:51,879 --> 00:15:55,400
They're really, oh, it's not loaded yet.

163
00:15:55,500 --> 00:15:55,840
Okay, there.

164
00:15:57,321 --> 00:16:08,705
There are really important cultural considerations for behavioral health care, and that is specifically around privacy, emphasizing dignity, and the power of community.

165
00:16:09,286 --> 00:16:12,327
So as for the importance of privacy,

166
00:16:13,221 --> 00:16:21,128
that within many of these listening sessions, many mentioned the culture of denial or culture of dismissal of challenges.

167
00:16:21,188 --> 00:16:32,016
And so it can be really hard to bring up the idea that I need help because the idea is, no, everyone's saying, no, you don't, or that's not a problem.

168
00:16:32,076 --> 00:16:35,779
So sort of internalizing that denial and dismissal.

169
00:16:35,839 --> 00:16:40,022
So it's really important that everything be very private and not just

170
00:16:41,059 --> 00:16:52,266
the normal privacy of a patient client or a patient provider, but also like how the services are named, you know, if you say, oh, I'm going to, where are you going?

171
00:16:53,246 --> 00:17:04,993
If you say your mom asks you where are you headed, you're going to, you know, if you have to obviously state a center that within its name is a mental health provider space, it can bring up that tension.

172
00:17:05,474 --> 00:17:07,255
And then the name of the service as well.

173
00:17:07,415 --> 00:17:07,995
So is it,

174
00:17:09,972 --> 00:17:18,074
Is it therapy or is it, you know, a personal growth session or, you know, just as an example.

175
00:17:19,055 --> 00:17:27,597
And then emphasizing dignity and that many of the participants mentioned the desire for belonging and cultural exchange.

176
00:17:27,657 --> 00:17:38,700
And this is, again, something that we've heard over and over again in many different types of listening sessions, but that their cultures are authentic, worthy of recognition and celebration.

177
00:17:39,927 --> 00:17:59,336
and that there's this opportunity to sort of lean into the ways that folks describe themselves and describe their cultures and to find ways that the way that we might understand mental health might resonate the way that they see their own health and their own mental health.

178
00:17:59,897 --> 00:18:06,860
And also that there's an opportunity here to partner with some faith groups for religiously competent care as well.

179
00:18:07,853 --> 00:18:12,696
since they are really trailers and anchors for some of our immigrant communities.

180
00:18:13,817 --> 00:18:35,532
And then finally, the power of community is really, many folks shared that this kind of open discussion with this honesty and the safety of community members really did help to reduce the taboo of these subjects and really feel like this was important to talk about, but that it just wasn't being talked about.

181
00:18:37,509 --> 00:18:55,236
And then the added piece of how family is so important, but there is this kind of disconnect between immigrant parents and their children who are growing up here and parents not really knowing how to connect with their children over the struggles that they might have in that in that process.

182
00:18:55,296 --> 00:19:04,320
So, you know, there's an opportunity here for alternative therapies for family bonding that might not be called family therapy, but something else.

183
00:19:06,727 --> 00:19:32,767
And so from this, really focusing on the three levels of care, the first being the promoting general wellness, updating and developing materials with culturally resonant language with our cultural linguistic communities that we reframe for the positive mental health care benefits and not the unburdening as I mentioned.

184
00:19:34,925 --> 00:19:46,332
and that we can support families bridge this cultural understanding between the cultures that they're coming from and the cultures that they're now living in here in Medford, but also for their children.

185
00:19:47,172 --> 00:19:50,674
And then the second level would be to prepare support systems.

186
00:19:51,155 --> 00:20:02,722
So creating community-led mental health first aid with also perhaps the faith-based communities that we can develop a database of culturally competent mental health resources.

187
00:20:02,782 --> 00:20:03,422
So if someone

188
00:20:04,676 --> 00:20:13,880
is asking for support in a certain language or from looking for someone from a particular background that we have that on hand to refer people to.

189
00:20:13,900 --> 00:20:23,784
And then we can also identify services available in languages other than English for a similar reason of someone looking for a service in a particular language.

190
00:20:24,965 --> 00:20:30,727
And then lastly, the third level is really within treatment and evaluating the treatment process.

191
00:20:31,510 --> 00:20:52,996
So we can review where institutions can support future providers of color or who speak languages other than English to diversify their workforce, because they are interested in hiring folks who speak languages other than English, but they haven't necessarily been supported in that career path.

192
00:20:53,496 --> 00:20:59,538
So really supporting through the employment development process as well.

193
00:21:00,107 --> 00:21:14,314
And then finally to the math, the intake and the assessment practices with cultural humility and really assessing, you know, what questions are being asked to understand a cultural background or cultural, or someone's culture.

194
00:21:14,334 --> 00:21:26,921
And that's through both, you know, here intake, especially because folks really shared the frustration of having to go through a therapy session and not really receiving any

195
00:21:27,695 --> 00:21:51,627
or feeling like they received any support because they spent most of the session explaining, you know, how their family is structured and the dynamics between their grandmother and their mother and how that, you know, it might be in one culture very common in that way and it's very quick to, it's a lot quicker to explain oneself and that she felt that she didn't really have the time to get to what she wanted to talk about.

196
00:21:53,323 --> 00:21:55,945
So yeah, thank you very much.

197
00:21:56,465 --> 00:21:59,108
And thank you for listening.

198
00:21:59,148 --> 00:22:00,208
Thank you for having me here.

199
00:22:00,729 --> 00:22:09,676
And I hope that these recommendations can also help with your commission and the projects and the initiatives that come forth.

200
00:22:10,276 --> 00:22:10,636
Thank you.

201
00:22:12,758 --> 00:22:13,539
Thank you, Andrienne.

